# Expense Reimbursement Policy — ground transport

> Module GroundTransport

> Using ExpenseDefs

E-5 legislates over two different units and they are kept in separate scopes.
E-5.1 and E-5.2 are about a rail ticket and turn on the class of travel;
E-5.3 is about a taxi or private hire **journey** ("£60 per journey") and turns
on where and when the journey ran. Merging them would invent a rule that E-5
does not contain, namely a single cap applying across both heads.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration enumeration RailClass:
  -- StandardClass
  -- FirstClass

declaration scope RailFare:
  input class_of_travel content RailClass
  input ticket_cost content money
  input standard_class_fare_same_day content money
  input pre_approval content ExpenseDefs.PreApproval
  input incurred_on content date
  internal is_standard_class content boolean
  internal is_first_class content boolean
  internal pre_approval_obtained content boolean
  output reimbursable_amount content money

declaration scope TaxiFare:
  input journey_cost content money
  input is_airport_journey content boolean
  input journey_start_hour content integer
  input journey_start_minute content integer
  internal minutes_after_midnight content integer
  output reimbursable_amount content money
```

There are no judgement inputs. `standard_class_fare_same_day` is a
counterfactual *price* — the standard class fare for the same journey booked on
the day of travel — which is a figure to be evidenced, like a salary, and not
something anyone exercises judgement over. That is what keeps E-5.2 out of
HYBRID.

E-5.3's time of day is taken as an hour and a minute rather than a `date`,
because Catala's `date` has no time component and E-5.3's carve-out turns on
the clock and not on the calendar.

## E-5.1 Standard class rail

| EXP-POL E-5.1 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:82)
|
| Standard class rail travel is reimbursable at actual cost.

Uncapped, and stated in its own right so that the contrast E-5.2 draws is
visible in the code: the definition is conditioned on the class of travel being
standard, and E-5.2 supplies the definition for the other value of the
enumeration. The two conditions are exhaustive and mutually exclusive over
`RailClass`, so exactly one applies and no `Conflict` can arise.

"At actual cost" makes the ticket cost the measure of the reimbursement, and
the cost of a ticket is a sum spent: it is nought or more. A negative ticket
cost is not a cost E-5.1 could be reimbursing, and on the definition below it
would be reimbursed as a negative amount — the Employee owing the Company for
having travelled. The scope therefore refuses it rather than paying it
backwards. The bound is E-5.1's own ("actual cost"), not a convenience: the
same figure is what E-5.2 measures against the standard class fare.

```catala
scope RailFare:
  assertion ticket_cost >= $0.00

  definition is_standard_class equals class_of_travel with pattern StandardClass

  label e5_1 definition reimbursable_amount
    under condition is_standard_class
    consequence equals ticket_cost
```

## E-5.2 First class rail

| EXP-POL E-5.2 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:84)
|
| First class rail travel is reimbursable only where the ticket cost does
| not exceed the standard class fare for the same journey booked on the day of
| travel, or where Pre-Approval has been obtained.

"Reimbursable only where" is a prohibition with two alternative releases, so it
is encoded as a base of nil for first class travel with a single exception
carrying the disjunction. The two gates are alternatives, not a hierarchy —
either suffices — which is why they are one exception with an `or` rather than
two nested ones.

`does not exceed` is `<=`: a first class ticket costing exactly the same-day
standard class fare is reimbursable.

The counterfactual fare E-5.2 names — "the standard class fare for the same
journey booked on the day of travel" — is a price, and a price is nought or
more. A negative figure describes no fare that could have been booked, so the
comparison E-5.2 requires cannot be made against it and the scope declines
rather than answering. The bound comes from the clause: it is the *fare* that
the ticket cost must not exceed, and the release E-5.2 grants turns on that
comparison alone.

The exception repeats its parent's condition `is_first_class`. Without it the
exception would fire for standard class travel too and conflict with E-5.1.

```catala
scope RailFare:
  assertion standard_class_fare_same_day >= $0.00

  definition is_first_class equals class_of_travel with pattern FirstClass

  definition pre_approval_obtained equals
    (output of ExpenseDefs.PreApprovalValid with {
       -- pre_approval: pre_approval
       -- expense_incurred_at: incurred_on
       -- expense_amount: ticket_cost
     }).valid

  label e5_2 definition reimbursable_amount
    under condition is_first_class
    consequence equals $0.00

  label e5_2_permitted exception e5_2 definition reimbursable_amount
    under condition
      is_first_class
      and (ticket_cost <= standard_class_fare_same_day
           or pre_approval_obtained)
    consequence equals ticket_cost
```

## E-5.3 Private hire and taxi

| EXP-POL E-5.3 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:88)
|
| Private hire and taxi journeys are reimbursable at actual cost up to
| £60 per journey, save that a journey to or from an airport before 06:00 or after
| 22:00 is reimbursable at actual cost without limit.

The "save that" limb is an exception within the same clause, so it is encoded as
an exception to the £60 cap rather than folded into it. Both of the carve-out's
requirements must hold: the journey must be to or from an airport **and** it
must fall before 06:00 or after 22:00. An airport journey at noon is capped, and
a non-airport journey at 03:00 is capped.

The boundaries are read strictly as the words run. "Before 06:00" excludes
06:00 itself, and "after 22:00" excludes 22:00 itself, so a journey starting at
exactly either time is capped at £60. That leaves the interval from 06:00 to
22:00 inclusive inside the cap.

**The hour and the minute are clock components and E-5.3 fixes their range.**
The carve-out is stated as two times of day, "before 06:00 or after 22:00", so
the operative fact is the clock time at which the journey began. A clock hour
of the day runs from 00 to 23 and a minute within the hour from 00 to 59;
`24:17` and `10:5000` are not times, and a journey cannot have started at one.
The bound is therefore a requirement of the clause — it is what makes
`minutes_after_midnight` a position within one day, which is the only thing the
comparison against 06:00 and 22:00 can mean — and not a programming
convenience. It is asserted on each component rather than on the derived total,
because it is each component that the clock constrains and because the
diagnostic then names the component the caller got wrong.

Unguarded, the composed figure escaped the day entirely: hour 10 with minute
5000 gives 5,600 minutes after midnight, which satisfies "after 22:00" and
reimburses an airport journey at noon without limit, and hour -1 likewise
satisfies "before 06:00". The reviewer recorded both against CE-0011 as the
same class of defect — an unguarded input domain — rather than as a separate
counterexample, because no prose fact pattern states a journey time in that
form.

The journey cost carries the same bound as the rail ticket cost above, for the
same reason: E-5.3 reimburses "actual cost", and a negative cost is not one.

```catala
scope TaxiFare:
  assertion journey_start_hour >= 0 and journey_start_hour <= 23

  assertion journey_start_minute >= 0 and journey_start_minute <= 59

  assertion journey_cost >= $0.00

  definition minutes_after_midnight equals
    journey_start_hour * 60 + journey_start_minute

  label e5_3 definition reimbursable_amount equals
    Money.min of journey_cost, $60.00

  label e5_3_airport exception e5_3 definition reimbursable_amount
    under condition
      is_airport_journey
      and (minutes_after_midnight < 6 * 60
           or minutes_after_midnight > 22 * 60)
    consequence equals journey_cost
```
